[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering the Command Pattern: Undo/Redo and Transactional Operations"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Command Pattern is a behavioral design pattern that turns a request into a stand-alone object containing all information about the request. This transformation lets you parameterize methods with different requests, delay or queue a request's execution, and support undoable operations."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of the Command Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Command: Declares an interface for executing an operation."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"ConcreteCommand: Defines a binding between a Receiver object and an action. Implements execute by invoking the operation(s) on the receiver."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Client: Creates a ConcreteCommand object and sets its receiver."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Invoker: Asks the command to carry out the request."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Receiver: Knows how to perform the operations associated with carrying out a request."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Undo/Redo Functionality"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To support undo and redo operations, the Command interface must be extended to include an undo method. Concrete commands will then store the state necessary to reverse their execution."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Command {
    execute(): void;
    undo(): void;
}

class TextEditor {
    private text: string = '';

    append(newText: string) {
        this.text += newText;
    }

    delete(length: number) {
        this.text = this.text.slice(0, -length);
    }

    getText(): string {
        return this.text;
    }
}

class AppendCommand implements Command {
    private addedText: string;

    constructor(private editor: TextEditor, text: string) {
        this.addedText = text;
    }

    execute() {
        this.editor.append(this.addedText);
    }

    undo() {
        this.editor.delete(this.addedText.length);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Building the Invoker with History Management"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"The invoker maintains a history stack of executed commands. When an undo action is requested, the last executed command is popped from the history stack and its undo method is invoked."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"class CommandHistory {
    private undoStack: Command[] = [];
    private redoStack: Command[] = [];

    execute(command: Command) {
        command.execute();
        this.undoStack.push(command);
        this.redoStack = []; // Clear redo stack on new action
    }

    undo() {
        const command = this.undoStack.pop();
        if (command) {
            command.undo();
            this.redoStack.push(command);
        }
    }

    redo() {
        const command = this.redoStack.pop();
        if (command) {
            command.execute();
            this.undoStack.push(command);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Transactional Operations (Macro Commands)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"A macro command groups multiple commands into a single transactional unit. If any part of a transaction fails, or if a global rollback is triggered, all commands within the macro can be undone in reverse order to maintain state consistency."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"class MacroCommand implements Command {
    private commands: Command[] = [];

    add(command: Command) {
        this.commands.push(command);
    }

    execute() {
        for (const cmd of this.commands) {
            cmd.execute();
        }
    }

    undo() {
        // Undo in reverse order
        for (let i = this.commands.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            this.commands[i].undo();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices and Considerations"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Memory Management: Long history stacks can consume significant memory. Implement a maximum history limit or garbage collection strategy for old commands."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deep vs Shallow Copies: Ensure that state stored inside commands is properly cloned if mutable objects are modified elsewhere."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Idempotency: Ensure that executing and undoing commands leaves the system in predictable, deterministic states."}]
